# Line endings are LOAD-BEARING for .cmd, not a style preference.
#
# cmd.exe re-reads a batch file by byte offset every time it takes a `goto`, and
# an LF-only file makes it land in the wrong place. That shows up either as
# "The system cannot find the batch label specified" or -- far worse -- as a
# block whose `exit /b 2` silently stops running, so a usage error prints its
# message and then reports SUCCESS. Both were observed in harmony/build.cmd
# before this file existed.
#
# Pinning it here rather than trusting core.autocrlf: a clone with
# core.autocrlf=input (the usual setting on a machine that also does Linux work)
# would otherwise check these out LF-only and hit exactly that.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# The opposite requirement: CRLF in a shell script puts a stray \r on the end of
# the shebang, and the kernel then looks for an interpreter that does not exist.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The vendored SQLite amalgamation is generated upstream code that is never
# edited here, and sqlite3.c alone is 270k lines. Keeping it out of diffs and
# language stats stops one dependency bump from burying an actual review.
third/sqlite3/sqlite3.c -diff linguist-vendored
third/sqlite3/sqlite3.h -diff linguist-vendored
third/rapidjson/include/** linguist-vendored
